import numpy as np

def kl_divergence(p, q):
    return np.sum(p * np.log2(p / q))   # weight each log-ratio term by p(x)

p = np.array([0.5, 0.3, 0.2])
q = np.array([0.3, 0.4, 0.3])
print(kl_divergence(p, q))   # matches scipy.stats.entropy(p, q, base=2)

sections.body:
Cross-entropy is not an independent quantity invented separately from entropy and KL divergence -- it decomposes algebraically into exactly those two pieces. Starting from the definition and inserting p(x)/p(x) = 1 inside the log (a standard algebraic trick):

H(P,Q) = -sum_x p(x)*log2(q(x)) = -sum_x p(x)*log2( p(x) * q(x)/p(x) ) = -sum_x p(x)*[ log2(p(x)) + log2(q(x)/p(x)) ] = -sum_x p(x)*log2(p(x)) - sum_x p(x)*log2(q(x)/p(x)) = H(P) + sum_x p(x)*log2(p(x)/q(x)) = H(P) + KL(P||Q).

This single identity is the reason cross-entropy is the loss function used to train every standard classifier, rather than KL divergence being used directly, and it's worth being completely explicit about why. In a supervised training setup, P is the empirical distribution of true labels in the data -- it is a fixed, given fact about the dataset, and critically, **it does not depend on the model's parameters at all**. Only Q, the model's predicted distribution, depends on the parameters being optimized. Since H(P) is therefore a constant with respect to those parameters, it contributes nothing to the gradient of the loss with respect to the model: d/d(theta) [ H(P,Q) ] = d/d(theta) [ H(P) + KL(P||Q) ] = 0 + d/d(theta) [ KL(P||Q) ], because the derivative of a constant is exactly zero. Minimizing H(P,Q) over the model's parameters and minimizing KL(P||Q) over those same parameters are therefore not just related -- they have identical gradients everywhere and land on exactly the same minimizing parameters. Cross-entropy is simply the more convenient of the two to compute in practice: it requires evaluating only -sum p(x)*log2(q(x)), never needing to separately compute the (irrelevant, constant) H(P) term at all, whereas computing KL(P||Q) directly would require calculating both H(P) and the log-ratio, extra work that changes nothing about where the optimization ends up.

```python
import numpy as np

def entropy(p):
    p = p[p > 0]
    return -np.sum(p * np.log2(p))

def kl_divergence(p, q):
    mask = p > 0
    return np.sum(p[mask] * np.log2(p[mask] / q[mask]))

def cross_entropy(p, q):
    mask = p > 0
    return -np.sum(p[mask] * np.log2(q[mask]))

p = np.array([0.5, 0.3, 0.2])   # a soft (non-one-hot) true distribution, for generality
q = np.array([0.4, 0.4, 0.2])

h_p, kl, h_pq = entropy(p), kl_divergence(p, q), cross_entropy(p, q)
print(f"H(P) = {h_p:.4f}, KL(P||Q) = {kl:.4f}, H(P)+KL(P||Q) = {h_p + kl:.4f}, H(P,Q) = {h_pq:.4f}")
# H(P) = 1.4855, KL(P||Q) = 0.0365, H(P)+KL(P||Q) = 1.5219, H(P,Q) = 1.5219
```

